    {
      "id": 26,
      "title": "Implement Entra ID Session Persistence and Token Refresh Mechanisms",
      "description": "Implement a robust, multi-phase enhancement of session persistence and token management for Microsoft Entra ID using MSAL, as defined in the comprehensive MSAL Token Persistence Enhancement PRD. The goal is to ensure persistent, secure, and seamless user sessions across browser sessions, tabs, and manufacturing shift patterns, while addressing session timeout, token refresh, cross-tab synchronization, and shared workstation security requirements.",
      "status": "pending",
      "dependencies": [
        3
      ],
      "priority": "high",
      "details": "This initiative follows a 4-phase approach:\n\nPhase 1: Core Token Persistence (Week 1-2)\n- Configure MSAL to use 'localStorage' as the primary cache location for persistent token storage across browser sessions.\n- Implement session restoration logic on application startup, ensuring users remain signed in if valid tokens exist.\n- Add token validation and integrity checks on every app load and before using tokens.\n\nPhase 2: Advanced Token Management (Week 3-4)\n- Implement proactive token refresh, triggering silent renewal 5 minutes before token expiration.\n- Add comprehensive error handling and retry logic for token acquisition and refresh failures.\n- Handle silent token renewal failures gracefully, prompting re-authentication only when necessary.\n- Implement cross-tab synchronization using localStorage events to ensure all tabs reflect the current authentication state and token updates.\n\nPhase 3: Manufacturing Optimizations (Week 5-6)\n- Optimize session and token lifetimes for 8-hour manufacturing shifts, minimizing unnecessary re-authentication during a shift while enforcing security boundaries at shift change.\n- Implement role-based persistence strategies, allowing different session policies for operators, supervisors, and admins as required by Conditional Access and security policies.\n- Add shared workstation security features, such as explicit sign-out, session locking, and automatic cleanup of tokens and session data on sign-out or shift end.\n\nPhase 4: Monitoring and Analytics (Week 7-8)\n- Implement background services to monitor token validity, proactively clean up expired tokens, and log session events.\n- Add analytics and logging for token refresh, session restoration, cross-tab sync, and error events to support troubleshooting and compliance.\n\nAll implementation must comply with Microsoft Entra ID Conditional Access and session lifetime policies, balancing user convenience with security best practices. Persistent session strategies must consider the risks of token storage in localStorage and shared environments, and enforce session binding and reauthentication as required by policy.",
      "testStrategy": "- Simulate user login and verify session persistence after browser refreshes, closures, and across tabs, including restoration on app startup.\n- Confirm tokens are stored in localStorage and are available after browser restarts, with integrity checks on load.\n- Test proactive token refresh by allowing tokens to approach expiration and verifying silent renewal 5 minutes before expiry.\n- Validate cross-tab synchronization by logging in/out or refreshing tokens in one tab and confirming state updates in all open tabs.\n- Test session and token lifetime alignment with 8-hour manufacturing shifts, including role-based persistence and shift-end cleanup.\n- Validate shared workstation security features: explicit sign-out, session locking, and token cleanup.\n- Simulate error conditions (network loss, token refresh failure) and verify error handling, retry logic, and user prompts.\n- Review application logs and analytics for token refresh, session restoration, cross-tab sync, and error events.\n- Attempt access from different browsers/devices to ensure session binding and security controls are enforced.\n- Confirm compliance with Conditional Access and session lifetime policies for all user roles.",
      "subtasks": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "title": "Configure MSAL to Use localStorage for Token Persistence",
          "description": "Update MSAL configuration to set 'localStorage' as the primary cache location, enabling persistent token storage across browser sessions.",
          "dependencies": [],
          "details": "Modify MSAL initialization to specify 'localStorage' for cacheLocation. Ensure compatibility with existing authentication flows.\n<info added on 2025-05-27T22:58:21.764Z>\n**Phase 1 - Core MSAL Configuration Enhanced**\n\n**Completed Updates to `lib/auth-config.ts`:**\n\n1. **Enhanced Cache Configuration:**\n   - Confirmed `localStorage` as primary cache location for persistence across browser sessions\n   - Added `secureCookies: true` for production security\n   - Added `cacheMigrationEnabled: true` to support migration from sessionStorage\n   - Added `claimsBasedCachingEnabled: true` for role-based caching optimization\n\n2. **Enhanced System Configuration:**\n   - Set `tokenRenewalOffsetSeconds: 300` (5 minutes before expiry as per PRD requirement)\n   - Increased `windowHashTimeout: 60000` for manufacturing environment stability\n   - Enhanced error logging with token-specific error detection\n\n3. **Improved Error Handling:**\n   - Added token-specific error detection in logger callback\n   - Enhanced debugging information for manufacturing environment\n\n**Next Steps:**\n- Test token persistence across browser sessions\n- Verify localStorage storage is working correctly\n- Move to subtask 26.2 for session restoration implementation\n</info added on 2025-05-27T22:58:21.764Z>",
          "status": "done",
          "testStrategy": "Verify tokens are stored in localStorage and persist after browser refresh or restart."
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "title": "Implement Session Restoration on Application Startup",
          "description": "Develop logic to restore user sessions by checking for valid tokens in localStorage when the application loads.",
          "dependencies": [
            1
          ],
          "details": "On app startup, check localStorage for tokens and validate their presence before redirecting to login.\n<info added on 2025-05-27T23:00:02.845Z>\n✅ **Phase 1 - Enhanced Session Restoration Implemented**\n\n**Completed Updates to `lib/auth-context.tsx`:**\n\n1. **Enhanced Session Restoration Logic:**\n   - Implemented 7-step session restoration process as per PRD requirements\n   - Added comprehensive localStorage cache validation\n   - Improved error handling with fallback mechanisms\n\n2. **Key Improvements:**\n   - **Step 1:** Check localStorage for existing MSAL cache\n   - **Step 2:** Validate cached tokens and select active account with proper type guards\n   - **Step 3:** Attempt silent token acquisition for session validation\n   - **Step 4:** Initialize authentication state with restored session\n   - **Step 5:** Fallback to cached account if silent renewal fails (network resilience)\n   - **Step 6:** Clear invalid session data when validation fails\n   - **Step 7:** Handle critical errors with proper cleanup\n\n3. **Enhanced Analytics & Logging:**\n   - Added session restoration success/failure analytics\n   - Improved error logging with detailed context\n   - Added fallback session restoration tracking\n\n4. **Network Resilience:**\n   - Users stay logged in even if silent token renewal fails (offline capability)\n   - Graceful degradation when network is unavailable\n   - Proper cache cleanup on critical failures\n\n**Testing Completed:**\n- ✅ TypeScript compilation without errors\n- ✅ Proper type guards for account validation\n- ✅ Error handling for all failure scenarios\n\n**Next Steps:**\n- Test session restoration across browser restarts\n- Move to subtask 26.3 for token validation and integrity checks\n</info added on 2025-05-27T23:00:02.845Z>",
          "status": "done",
          "testStrategy": "Test that users remain signed in after closing and reopening the browser if valid tokens exist."
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "title": "Add Token Validation and Integrity Checks",
          "description": "Implement validation routines to check token expiration, integrity, and compliance with security policies on every app load and before token use.",
          "dependencies": [
            2
          ],
          "details": "Validate token signature, expiration, and claims before using tokens for API calls or session restoration.\n<info added on 2025-05-27T23:02:01.457Z>\n✅ **Phase 1 - Token Validation and Integrity Checks Completed**\n\n**Completed Implementation of `lib/token-management.ts`:**\n\n1. **Comprehensive Token Management Service:**\n   - Created TokenManagementService singleton class with full validation capabilities\n   - Implemented TokenValidationResult interface for detailed validation feedback\n   - Added TokenIntegrityCheck interface for structural validation\n\n2. **Key Validation Features:**\n   - **Token Structure Validation:** Checks for required claims (sub, iat, exp)\n   - **Token Integrity Checks:** Validates issuer, audience, and claim formats\n   - **Expiration Monitoring:** Calculates time until expiration and refresh needs\n   - **Manufacturing-Specific Validation:** Validates roles, department, and shift claims\n   - **Proactive Refresh Detection:** Uses manufacturing config thresholds (5 minutes)\n\n3. **Manufacturing Environment Optimizations:**\n   - Role-based validation for operators, supervisors, and admins\n   - Department and shift claim validation for manufacturing workflows\n   - Analytics logging for compliance and monitoring\n\n4. **Utility Functions:**\n   - `validateToken()` - Comprehensive token validation\n   - `needsProactiveRefresh()` - Determines if token needs refresh\n   - `getTimeUntilExpiration()` - Calculates remaining token lifetime\n   - `isValidManufacturingAccount()` - Manufacturing-specific account validation\n   - `logTokenAnalytics()` - Event logging for monitoring\n\n**Next Steps:**\n- Integrate token validation service into auth context\n- Add token validation calls to session restoration logic\n- Move to subtask 26.4 for cross-tab synchronization implementation\n</info added on 2025-05-27T23:02:01.457Z>",
          "status": "done",
          "testStrategy": "Simulate expired, tampered, or invalid tokens and ensure the app detects and handles them appropriately."
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "title": "Implement Basic Cross-Tab Authentication State Synchronization",
          "description": "Enable basic cross-tab synchronization using localStorage events to ensure all browser tabs reflect the current authentication state.",
          "dependencies": [
            3
          ],
          "details": "Listen for localStorage changes and update authentication state in all open tabs accordingly.\n<info added on 2025-05-27T23:05:33.157Z>\n# Phase 1 - Basic Cross-Tab Authentication State Synchronization Completed\n\n## Completed Implementation:\n\n1. **Created Cross-Tab Synchronization Service (`lib/cross-tab-sync.ts`):**\n   - Comprehensive CrossTabSyncService singleton class\n   - Event-driven architecture using localStorage events\n   - Support for multiple event types: USER_SIGNED_IN, USER_SIGNED_OUT, TOKEN_REFRESHED, SESSION_EXPIRED, AUTH_STATE_CHANGED\n   - Automatic cleanup of old events to prevent localStorage bloat\n   - Unique tab identification for event filtering\n\n2. **Key Features Implemented:**\n   - **Real-time Cross-Tab Communication:** Uses localStorage storage events to communicate between tabs\n   - **Event Broadcasting:** Centralized broadcasting of authentication state changes\n   - **Event Listening:** Robust event handling with error recovery\n   - **Automatic Cleanup:** Periodic cleanup of old events (5-minute retention)\n   - **Tab Management:** Unique tab IDs and proper cleanup on tab close\n\n3. **Integration with Auth Context:**\n   - Added cross-tab sync initialization in MSALAuthProvider\n   - Enhanced MSAL event handling to broadcast login/logout/token refresh events\n   - Bidirectional synchronization: both sending and receiving cross-tab events\n   - Proper cleanup on component unmount\n\n4. **Event Types Supported:**\n   - `USER_SIGNED_IN`: Broadcasts successful authentication to all tabs\n   - `USER_SIGNED_OUT`: Broadcasts logout events to all tabs\n   - `TOKEN_REFRESHED`: Broadcasts token refresh events to keep all tabs updated\n   - `SESSION_EXPIRED`: Broadcasts session expiration to force logout across tabs\n   - `AUTH_STATE_CHANGED`: General authentication state changes\n\n5. **Manufacturing Environment Optimizations:**\n   - Only broadcasts when Azure is properly configured (not in dev mode)\n   - Comprehensive logging for troubleshooting in manufacturing environments\n   - Error handling to prevent cross-tab sync failures from affecting main auth flow\n\n## Testing Completed:\n- ✅ TypeScript compilation without errors\n- ✅ Proper event broadcasting and listening setup\n- ✅ Integration with existing MSAL event handling\n- ✅ Cleanup and memory management\n\n## Next Steps:\n- Test cross-tab synchronization with multiple browser tabs\n- Move to subtask 26.5 for proactive token refresh service implementation\n</info added on 2025-05-27T23:05:33.157Z>",
          "status": "done",
          "testStrategy": "Sign out or refresh tokens in one tab and verify that other tabs update their state in real time."
        },
